WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.740
Security-relevant logging should focus on events that help authorized teams understand meaningful activity.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:24.440
Authentication successes and failures, authorization failures, access-denied decisions, administrative actions, permission changes, data exports, sensitive record access, configuration changes, and high-impact business actions often deserve attention.

3
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.040
The level of detail should be appropriate.

4
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:40.180
For authentication, a system may need to know that a sign-in succeeded or failed, which application component handled it, which safe identity reference was involved, and whether rate limits or policy decisions were triggered.

5
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:46.280
It should not log passwords, tokens, cookies, or sensitive identity assertions.

6
00:00:46.560 --> 00:01:04.960
Useful context includes timestamps, ExampleRequestId, ExampleCorrelationId, ExampleUserId, ExampleTenantId, ExampleActionName, service name, route pattern, outcome, status category, and safe object references such as ExampleOrderId.

7
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:10.660
The point is to preserve enough context for investigation while avoiding raw sensitive data.

8
00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.220
Application lifecycle events can be important.

9
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:30.080
Startup, shutdown, configuration validation failures, migrations, dependency failures, queue processing errors, background job failures, and deployment transitions help operators understand whether the system is healthy and whether a change affected behavior.

10
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:39.880
Validation failures, rate-limit events, repeated operational failures, and suspicious workflow patterns can produce useful signals at a defensive level.

11
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:45.020
The goal is not to log every typo or ordinary user mistake as an incident.

12
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.960
The goal is to capture patterns that help teams distinguish normal friction from behavior that deserves investigation.

13
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.760
Logs should support investigation without becoming noisy.

14
00:01:54.860 --> 00:02:00.780
If logs omit decision-critical context, responders may not be able to reconstruct what happened.

15
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.480
If logs record every low-value detail, important events disappear into volume and cost.

16
00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:12.340
Review log usefulness during incidents, false positives, and support escalations.
